ation:</strong> healthcare ecosystem and value chain; resource organisation/planning/location; service-provision models; chronic-care services; technology and health.</li>
    <li><strong>Management and decision-making:</strong> public-health strategy, health and society, epidemiology and healthcare research.</li>
    <li><strong>Digital health technologies:</strong> major systems including HIS, LIS, PACS, FIS and EHR.</li>
    <li><strong>Standards and interoperability:</strong> LOINC, HL7-CDA, HL7-FHIR, SNOMED CT, ICD-10, CPT and openEHR.</li>
    <li><strong>Clinical documentation:</strong> conceptual framework and core bioinformatics concepts.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Management, personal and social skills</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Integrate into healthcare-system organisations and clinical/epidemiological research teams.</li>
    <li>Collaborate in the design and programming of AI-project components and tools in the healthcare domain.</li>
    <li>Implement responsible capture, acquisition, manipulation and filtering of health data.</li>
    <li>Apply good practices in data handling in accordance with applicable published standards.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="footer">IFCD104 · Unofficial English translation for portfolio review</p>
</section>

<section class="page">
  <h2>Assessment of learning</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Assessment is theoretical and practical, systematic and continuous throughout each module and at the end of the course.</li>
    <li>An initial diagnostic assessment may be used to establish learners' starting level.</li>
    <li>Methods and instruments must appropriately verify the specified learning outcomes and support reliable, valid assessment.</li>
    <li>Each assessment instrument must include a corresponding correction/scoring system with clear measurement criteria.</li>
    <li>The final outcome is expressed as <strong>Pass / Not Pass</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
